  "data": {
    "text": "Lateral Prefrontal Cortex Contributes to Fluid\nIntelligence Through Multinetwork Connectivity\n\nMichael W. Cole, Takuya Ito, and Todd S. Braver\nBRAIN CONNECTIVITY — 2015 — Mary Ann Liebert, Inc.\nDOI: 10.1089/brain.2015.0357\n\nOur ability to effectively adapt to novel circumstances—as measured by general fluid intelligence—has recently been tied to the global connectivity of lateral prefrontal cortex (LPFC).\n\nOne hundred twenty-one participants (70 female; mean age = 23, range = 18–40) were recruited from the undergraduate population at Washington University or surrounding communities and received financial remuneration. The experiment was approved by the Washington University Institutional Review Board. The same 94 participants used in Cole et al. (2012) were used here.\n\nWhole-brain images were acquired on a 3 Tesla Allegra System (Siemens, Erlangen, Germany). Structural images were acquired using a T1-weighted sequence. Functional images were acquired using an asymmetric spin-echo echo-planar sequence sensitive to BOLD (TR = 2360 msec, TE = 50 msec, flip = 90°). Each scanning run gave 149 sets of brain volumes (32 contiguous, 4-mm-thick axial images, 4 · 4-mm in-plane resolution).\n\nFunctional images were extracted from the rest periods between task blocks of an N-back task. We used rest images that were 14.16 sec or more past the end of every task block and 4.72 sec or fewer into the start of every task block. The mean signal amplitude for each rest block was subtracted for each voxel before rest block concatenation. There were 3 rest blocks per run, resulting in 35 rest frames per run (after removing the first 5 frames of each run). There were 8.26 min of rest total across the six runs per participant.\n\nResting-state functional images were further preprocessed using AFNI with the following steps (in order): (1) slice-time correction, (2) removal of the first five images from each run to reach steady state, (3) rigid body motion correction, (4) removal of a set of nuisance regressors, including signals from the ventricles, deep white matter, whole-brain average, motion correction parameters, and first derivatives, (5) additional removal of local white matter nuisance regressors on a voxel-wise basis (ANATICOR) using 16-mm radii spheres, (6) band-pass filtering (0.009 < f(t) < 0.08), and (7) smoothing with a 6-mm spherical dilation of gray matter voxels.\n\nFollowing global brain connectivity processing, but before group analysis, the images were transformed into atlas space (Talairach and Tournoux, 1988)."
